{
  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n## 1) Build the task spec cumulatively from all user turns\n- Start with a running set of **user-stated facts and constraints**; update it after every new user message.\n- Treat later user messages as **state updates**: they can add constraints, complete an earlier partial description, or narrow the target.\n- Combine new information with earlier facts unless the user explicitly overrides or replaces something.\n- Separate **unknown so far** from **no longer relevant**: once the user supplies the missing detail, earlier uncertainty becomes stale.\n- Use the **latest cumulative user state** to define the active problem, not the most recent turn in isolation.\n\n## 2) Recompute the target before judging the assistant\n- Re-evaluate the assistant against the **full accumulated specification** each time the user adds information.\n- A response can be locally correct under an earlier partial spec and still become wrong once the conversation expands.\n- Before flagging alignment, ask: has the user changed the quantity to compute, the scope, or the endpoint of the chain?\n- If a later user fact changes what \u201cthe answer\u201d means, the earlier answer should be judged against the updated target, not preserved as correct-by-default.\n- Separate **math validity** from **task validity**: correct arithmetic does not count if it solves the wrong version of the problem.\n\n## 3) Track which facts are additive, corrective, or decisive\n- Not every new user detail is equally important: some are merely contextual, others change the final answer, and some explicitly correct prior assumptions.\n- When a later message adds a relation, total, or already-known amount, treat it as part of the current state, not as a side remark.\n- If a new fact makes the problem solvable, the analyzer should switch from \u201cinsufficient information\u201d reasoning to direct evaluation.\n- When the user restates or reinforces a constraint, treat that as evidence the constraint is stable unless contradicted.\n- Distinguish **answer-changing updates** from **non-changing but relevant updates**; both matter for state tracking, but only some alter the final output.\n\n## 4) Check whether every new user fact is actually integrated\n- A common failure mode is the assistant acknowledging a new detail but continuing as if it were absent.\n- Verify that each new quantity, entity, category, or relation is used in the reasoning path where it belongs.\n- If the user introduces multiple groups or components, ensure the assistant reconciles them when the task asks for a combined total or final state.\n- Look for stale carry-forward state: earlier partial values may need to be reused, revised, or discarded after later disclosure.\n- If the assistant mentions a fact but does not let it affect the computation, scope, or conclusion, treat that as missed integration.\n\n## 5) Diagnose stale-scope answers and anchoring\n- Flag cases where the assistant stays locked on an earlier interpretation after the user has revised or completed the task.\n- Prefer identifying the exact stale link: what assumption, scope, or endpoint failed to update?\n- If the assistant asks for more information after the accumulated user messages already determine the answer, that is a state-tracking error, not prudent caution.\n- Unsupported branches and speculative interpretations should be treated as removable context, not as stable background.\n- When later turns invalidate a prior solution path, compact away the outdated conclusion so it does not bias the next analysis.\n\n## 6) Separate intermediate validity from final correctness\n- Track intermediate quantities, subresults, and partial derivations independently from the final requested output.\n- A correct intermediate step should be marked aligned only if it still fits the full accumulated spec.\n- If the assistant solves only a subproblem or an earlier stage of the chain, mark it as incomplete even when the computation is right.\n- Do not overcredit \u201ccorrect by luck\u201d: a numerically correct result can still be a reasoning divergence if it relies on an unsupported assumption.\n- Distinguish aligned intermediate work, incomplete finalization, stale reasoning, and outright unsupported inference.\n\n## 7) Verify chain integrity link by link\n- For multi-step or relational tasks, check every link explicitly: user fact \u2192 derived relation \u2192 updated target \u2192 final answer.\n- Do not let a correct subtotal or local transformation stand in for completion of the full chain.\n- When a later user message changes the target, re-check whether earlier \u201cremainder,\u201d \u201ccomplement,\u201d or subtotal logic is still the right endpoint.\n- A response can be internally consistent at each step yet still fail if one link is missing, stale, or aimed at the wrong endpoint.\n- Reduce the user\u2019s constraints to the simplest implied chain and verify the assistant followed the whole chain, not just the nearest segment.\n\n## 8) Treat explicit user structure as decisive; inferred patterns as provisional\n- Prefer direct user-stated relationships over earlier guesses or pattern extrapolations.\n- A concrete rule or definition from the user should replace any tentative inference built from examples or partial wording.\n- If the user later supplies the missing rule, re-evaluate earlier assumptions from scratch rather than patching them locally.\n- Separate \u201cthe derivation works under an assumed pattern\u201d from \u201cthe problem is actually specified that way.\u201d\n- If the assistant invents a relationship the user never confirmed, flag it as an unsupported extrapolation even if the math is coherent.\n\n## 9) Watch for unnecessary clarification-seeking and overextension\n- Treat clarification requests as a last resort when the accumulated user spec is already sufficient.\n- If the assistant keeps asking after the user has already supplied enough information, flag that as divergence.\n- Be alert to branch proliferation, alternate scenarios, or broad digressions after the user has narrowed the task; that often signals lingering uncertainty rather than analysis.\n- When the user has constrained the problem enough to compute the answer, further \u201cwhat if\u201d exploration is noise.\n- The analyzer should prefer consolidation around the latest constraints over continued speculation.\n\n## 10) Use compact correction: keep only reusable truth\n- When later user facts supersede earlier speculation, preserve the validated facts and usable intermediate relations, not the outdated framing.\n- Remove the wrong derived path so the next turn starts from the cleanest supported state.\n- Keep only information that remains useful after refinement; discard any interpretation the newest disclosure disconfirms.\n- Compacting is about preventing stale context from biasing future analysis, not about preserving every branch.\n- The next evaluation should begin from the updated user state, with obsolete conclusions removed.\n\n## 11) Be precise about the issue category\n- Use the smallest accurate diagnosis: missed integration, stale-scope reasoning, unsupported assumption, incomplete finalization, or aligned intermediate work.\n- If the assistant answered an earlier subquestion instead of the final composite task, mark it incomplete rather than broadly wrong.\n- If the final answer conflicts with any stated user constraint, explicitly flag the mismatch rather than accepting partial correctness.\n- Prefer naming the missing link, stale assumption, or ignored update over generic labels like \u201cvague\u201d or \u201cincomplete.\u201d\n- The best diagnosis is the one that explains exactly how the assistant failed to track the evolving user state.",
  "version": 3,
  "history": [
    "",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n## 1) Build the task spec incrementally from user turns only\n- Construct the task spec strictly from the user\u2019s messages, not from assistant guesses or requests.\n- Treat each new user disclosure as a potential **state change**: update the spec, then re-evaluate the whole conversation against the revised spec.\n- If later user text resolves earlier ambiguity, overwrite the old uncertainty rather than carrying it forward as if it still applies.\n- Distinguish **\u201cunknown so far\u201d** from **\u201cnot needed\u201d**: a missing detail can become required once the user supplies the rest of the chain.\n- When the user adds a late constraint, do not treat earlier assistant \u201cneed more info\u201d language as authoritative; it becomes outdated if the user has now provided the missing piece.\n\n## 2) Re-evaluate against the full accumulated spec, not the latest fragment\n- Compare the assistant\u2019s response to the **latest complete user-stated requirement set**, not just the most recent turn.\n- Track all user-stated constraints together so omissions and missed scaling/finality issues become visible.\n- For sharded disclosures, earlier correct reasoning may become incomplete or obsolete after later turns; judge final correctness using the full accumulated spec.\n- Verify the answer against the **entire chain of user-provided transformations/relationships**, not only the last numeric statement or the most recent local pattern.\n- If a later user message changes the interpretation of an earlier response, treat the earlier response as obsolete for final-evaluation purposes, even if it was locally correct at the time.\n\n## 3) Separate intermediate work from the final objective\n- Track intermediate quantities, mappings, and partial totals separately from the final requested output.\n- A correct partial derivation should be marked as aligned only if it still remains valid after later disclosures.\n- Do not overvalue incidental correct details that do not contribute to the final task.\n- If the assistant stops at a valid sub-result but not the asked-for target, mark it as **insufficient rather than incorrect**.\n- Preserve validated intermediate relationships, but do not let them be mistaken for completion.\n\n## 4) Look for missed integration of newly supplied constraints\n- Check whether each new user detail is actually folded into the computation/path, rather than merely acknowledged.\n- A common failure pattern is the assistant continuing as if the task is incomplete after the user has already provided all required components.\n- When the user adds a quantity, factor, component, or relationship, verify that the final reasoning uses it exactly once and in the right place.\n- If the user says \u201cfor each of N\u2026,\u201d watch for the need to apply a global multiplier or analogous scope-expanding constraint.\n- Flag stale carry-forward state: intermediate values may need to be reused, updated, or discarded depending on the new user disclosure.\n\n## 5) Treat unnecessary clarification-seeking as a mismatch when the spec is already sufficient\n- \u201cNeeds clarification\u201d should be a last resort, not the default diagnosis.\n- If the user\u2019s accumulated statements already determine the answer, further questioning or waiting for more detail is a conversational-state error, not helpful caution.\n- Recognize when later user statements disambiguate an earlier vague phrase; the analyzer should prefer the explicit later relationship over earlier semantic uncertainty.\n- If the assistant keeps asking for information after the user has supplied it, flag that as divergence from the evolving conversation state.\n\n## 6) Diagnose overextension, side-paths, and unsupported assumptions\n- Separate genuine ambiguity from unnecessary elaboration: extra branches, alternate interpretations, dates, serial numbers, or reference-date digressions are noise when the user only needs a direct derivation.\n- Flag overgeneralization when the assistant expands a simple user-provided relationship into a broader family of possibilities the conversation no longer requires.\n- Even if a numeric result is locally valid, unsupported assumptions should still be flagged because they can mask missing user constraints.\n- Prefer identifying the specific omitted link or misplaced assumption, rather than broadly saying the answer is incomplete or vague.\n\n## 7) Check chain integrity link by link\n- When the task depends on chained relations, verify each link explicitly: entity-to-entity, quantity-to-quantity, or step-to-step.\n- Make sure the analyzer checks the assistant\u2019s intermediate mappings before judging the final result.\n- For composed constraints, reduce them to the simplest implied computation path and see whether the assistant follows that full chain.\n- A response can be partially right at each step yet still fail if one link is missing, stale, or reinterpreted incorrectly.\n\n## 8) Use compact correction / S2-style rewriting to preserve only valid structure\n- If the assistant anchored on an irrelevant branch, prefer compacting to the true user-stated spec and the validated intermediate relationships.\n- Remove premature conclusions and outdated uncertainty so the next turn can continue from the correct partial model.\n- Preserve what is still valid; discard exploratory detours that no longer match the user\u2019s updated requirements.\n- The goal is to prevent the analyzer from carrying forward wrong state, not to preserve every conversational branch.\n\n## 9) Phrase findings with precision about status\n- Distinguish clearly between:\n  - aligned intermediate work,\n  - obsolete reasoning,\n  - insufficient finalization,\n  - and outright divergence.\n- Use the minimal missing link as the core issue when possible: identify exactly what was omitted, delayed, or not updated.\n- If earlier work is correct only under an old spec, say so explicitly instead of treating it as still valid for the final evaluation.\n- Keep the analysis anchored to user requirements, not to the assistant\u2019s internal narrative about uncertainty.\n\n## 10) Common turn-order pitfalls to watch for\n- Later user disclosures can convert \u201cunknown\u201d into \u201crequired\u201d; re-evaluate after every shard.\n- A response acceptable before a later turn may become wrong once the spec expands.\n- A correct subtotal is not a correct final answer.\n- A clarification request can become an error once the answer is already determined by the accumulated user messages.\n- A locally correct relationship can still be irrelevant if it does not contribute to the final task.",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n## 1) Rebuild the task spec cumulatively from user-only evidence\n- Extract constraints from **all user turns**, not just the latest one, and update the spec after each new disclosure.\n- Treat later user messages as potential **state changes**: they can resolve ambiguity, add requirements, or change the final target.\n- Distinguish **unknown so far** from **not needed**; missing information may become required once the user supplies more context.\n- If a later user statement completes or revises an earlier partial description, overwrite the older interpretation rather than carrying both forward.\n- Do not let assistant \u201cneed more info\u201d language override later user clarification; once the user has supplied the missing piece, the prior uncertainty is stale.\n\n## 2) Re-evaluate the whole conversation against the full accumulated spec\n- Judge each assistant turn against the **latest complete user-stated requirement set**, not only the most recent fragment.\n- Earlier reasoning that was locally valid can become obsolete after later user disclosures; re-check it against the updated spec.\n- When the user adds a global condition or aggregate constraint, verify that all earlier inferences still fit that condition.\n- For chained or incremental disclosures, rebuild one consistent task spec before deciding what the assistant got right or wrong.\n- A response that matched an earlier partial spec may still be a divergence once the conversation expands.\n\n## 3) Separate intermediate validity from final completion\n- Track partial derivations, intermediate quantities, and validated subresults separately from the requested final output.\n- A correct intermediate step should only be marked aligned if it remains valid under the full accumulated spec.\n- Flag **intermediate-only** progress when the assistant computes something useful but stops short of the user\u2019s final requested quantity.\n- Do not overcredit \u201ccorrect by luck\u201d: a numerically right result is still a reasoning divergence if it rests on an invented or unsupported rule.\n- Distinguish clearly between aligned intermediate work, insufficient finalization, obsolete reasoning, and outright divergence.\n\n## 4) Treat user-supplied structure as decisive; treat inferred patterns as provisional\n- When the user provides an explicit relationship, comparative statement, or aggregate condition, prefer that over any earlier pattern inference.\n- Pattern extrapolation from examples is only a hypothesis unless the user explicitly stated the rule.\n- If a later user message converts an underspecified problem into a solvable one, the analyzer should switch from speculative interpretation to direct evaluation.\n- When a new fact enables a direct solution, prefer the direct path over continued sequence inference or abstract branching.\n- Separate \u201cmath checks out under an assumed rule\u201d from \u201cthe problem is actually specified\u201d; both must be satisfied for full alignment.\n\n## 5) Check whether every new user fact is actually integrated\n- A common failure mode is the assistant acknowledging a new detail but continuing as if it were not present.\n- Verify that each new user constraint is used exactly where it belongs in the reasoning path, not merely mentioned.\n- When the user adds an entity, quantity, category, or relation, check whether the assistant updated the computation and scope accordingly.\n- If the conversation introduces multiple groups or entities, ensure the assistant accounts for the necessary aggregation or reconciliation across them when the user asks for a total.\n- Watch for stale carry-forward state: earlier partial values may need to be reused, updated, or discarded after a later disclosure.\n\n## 6) Diagnose anchoring and unsupported assumptions\n- Look for cases where the assistant remains locked on an earlier interpretation after the user has revised or completed the task.\n- Flag any conclusion that depends on an assumption the user never confirmed, especially if later user text narrows or changes the allowed interpretation.\n- Unsupported branches should be treated as removable context for S2-style compaction, not as valid background.\n- Prefer identifying the exact missing or stale link rather than broadly labeling the answer vague or incomplete.\n- If the assistant keeps asking for information after the accumulated user messages already determine the answer, that is a state-tracking error, not prudent caution.\n\n## 7) Verify chain integrity link by link\n- For tasks with derived relationships, check each link explicitly: entity-to-entity, quantity-to-quantity, step-to-step.\n- Do not let a correct subtotal or isolated relation stand in for completion of the full chain.\n- When later user messages change the final target, re-check whether earlier \u201cremainder,\u201d \u201ccomplement,\u201d or subtotal logic is still the right objective.\n- A response can be locally correct at every step yet still fail if one link is missing, stale, or reinterpreted incorrectly.\n- Reduce composed constraints to the simplest implied path and verify the assistant follows the entire chain, not just the nearest segment.\n\n## 8) Recognize unnecessary clarification-seeking and overextension\n- Treat clarification requests as a last resort when the accumulated user spec is already sufficient.\n- If the assistant asks for more detail after the user has already constrained the problem enough, flag that as divergence.\n- Separate genuine ambiguity from extra speculative elaboration: alternate branches, digressions, and broad scenario lists are noise when the user asked for a direct derivation.\n- Overgeneralization is an error when the assistant expands a narrow user-stated relationship into a broader family of possibilities the conversation no longer needs.\n\n## 9) Use compact correction: keep only reusable truth\n- When later user facts supersede earlier speculation, preserve only the validated subcomputations and discard the outdated framing.\n- The goal of S2-style compaction is to prevent wrong context from biasing later analysis, not to retain every branch.\n- Keep intermediate facts only if they remain useful after refinement; remove any inferred interpretation that the newest disclosure disconfirms.\n- If earlier reasoning becomes obsolete, identify the exact step that should be compacted away so the next turn starts from the correct partial model.\n\n## 10) Be precise about what kind of issue occurred\n- Use the smallest accurate diagnosis: aligned intermediate work, obsolete reasoning, insufficient finalization, unsupported assumption, or missed integration.\n- If the assistant answered an intermediate subproblem instead of the final composite task, mark it as incomplete even if the arithmetic is correct.\n- If the final answer conflicts with any stated user constraint, explicitly flag the mismatch rather than accepting the derivation because parts of it were right.\n- In sharded or incremental conversations, the analyzer should state how later disclosures constrain earlier quantities or interpretations.\n- Precision matters more than generic fault labels; name the missing link or stale assumption whenever possible."
  ],
